# Exam track — Theme: Describing a Picture — Sub-lesson 1: General Description
#
# The second picture theme, and the counterpart to Schueberfouer: where that
# photo is a summer funfair, this one is a WINTER city street — the Groussgaass
# (Grand-Rue) pedestrian zone in Luxembourg City. The contrast is deliberate:
# the seasonal inference in 03 lands on the opposite answer, so the hedging
# frames get exercised rather than memorised.
#
# The photo: a cobbled pedestrian street seen down its length, tall buildings
# with shops and cafés left and right, a woman in a white jacket and red scarf
# walking in the foreground, a child in pink further back in the middle, a
# construction hoarding on the left, bare trees and cars far away at the end.
#
# The photo's coverage is split across three sub-lessons, one per exam task:
#   01 — WHERE + WHAT: naming the place, the objects and their positions, and
#        what the people are doing.                    ← this file
#   02 — WHO:  the people, 1–2 of them described visually.
#   03 — WHEN: the weather, and a hedged guess at the season and time of day.
#
# PURE DESCRIPTION. No @question examiner prompts and no first-person opinion,
# preference or feeling — those belong to the topic themes. A depicted person's
# visible state is fine: that is observation. Rule of thumb — if a stranger
# could not check the sentence against the photo, it does not belong here.
#
# Not authored, for failing exactly that test: the shop *names* legible on the
# signs. A brand name is neither Luxembourgish vocabulary nor checkable
# description, which is why the source description's `e Chantier, dee seet dass
# Ladurée gebaut gëtt` ships here as the plain `Lénks ass e Chantier.`
#
# Vocabulary and grammar are held to A1–A2 deliberately, same as the other
# picture theme: present tense, simple main clauses, spatial adverbs +
# prepositions, no attributive adjectives on masculine or neuter nouns.
#
# This file NAMES THE PLACE, which is the examiner's first question and the gap
# a scene-elements-only file leaves: d'Foussgängerzon, d'Groussgaass, de Zentrum.
# LOD lists Groussgaass as the Luxembourgish name of the Grand-Rue, so it is
# safe as a named place.
#
# Nouns are taught in the number the content actually uses (d'Butteker, d'Beem,
# d'Autoen as plurals) — an unused singular is a free Element standing between
# the learner and the 100% gate.
#
# The @fill blocks take frames from the library in
# .claude/memory/picture-description-theme.md that the Schueberfouer theme left
# unused: the place-noun frame, the two-coordinated-nouns inventory, the
# additive `Ausserdeem` listing, and the background inventory. See the skill's
# references/content-contract.md for the mechanized bounds.

@lesson P2.01 "Groussgaass: General Description"

# Pre-cropped to 16:9 and encoded as WebP at 880px — see
# .claude/memory/picture-description-theme.md for the pipeline. @image-alt stays:
# it is the a11y text, and the caption of the placeholder frame if the file is
# ever missing.
@image "/assets/exam/picture/groussgaass/img/groussgaass.webp"
@image-alt "A cobbled pedestrian shopping street in a city on a grey winter day: tall buildings with shops on the left and right, a woman in a white jacket and red scarf walking in the foreground, a child in pink further back, a construction hoarding on the left, bare trees at the far end."

# --- Talking about a picture ---
@word d'Bild = the picture
@word gesinn = to see

# --- Naming the place ---
@word d'Stad = the city
@word d'Strooss = the street
@word d'Foussgängerzon = the pedestrian zone
@word d'Groussgaass = the Grand-Rue
@word de Zentrum = the centre

# --- What is in the street ---
@word d'Gebaier = the buildings
@word d'Butteker = the shops
@word d'Caféen = the pubs
@word de Chantier = the construction site
@word d'Stroosseluuchten = the street lamps
@word d'Autoen = the cars
@word d'Beem = the trees
@word héich = tall

# --- Positions ---
@word lénks = on the left
@word riets = on the right
@word hannen = at the back

# --- People and what they do ---
@word d'Leit = the people
@word akafen = to do the shopping

@sentence
@lu Dëst ass e Bild an der Stad.
@en This is a picture in the city.
@distractor-lu Duerf
@distractor-lu Bësch
@distractor-en village
@distractor-en forest

@sentence
@lu Et si vill Leit op enger Strooss.
@en There are a lot of people on a street.
@distractor-lu keng
@distractor-lu Autoen
@distractor-en no
@distractor-en cars

@sentence
@lu Et ass eng Foussgängerzon am Zentrum.
@en It is a pedestrian zone in the centre.
@distractor-lu Strooss
@distractor-lu Buttek
@distractor-en street
@distractor-en shop

@sentence
@lu D'Leit akafen an de Butteker.
@en The people are shopping in the shops.
@distractor-lu spillen
@distractor-lu Caféen
@distractor-en playing
@distractor-en pubs

@sentence
@lu Lénks ass e Chantier.
@en On the left there is a construction site.
@distractor-lu riets
@distractor-lu Gebai
@distractor-en right
@distractor-en building

@sentence
@lu D'Gebaier sinn héich an al.
@en The buildings are tall and old.
@distractor-lu kleng
@distractor-lu jonk
@distractor-en small
@distractor-en young

@sentence
@lu Hannen an der Strooss gesinn ech Beem an Autoen.
@en At the back of the street I see trees and cars.
@distractor-lu vir
@distractor-lu Butteker
@distractor-en front
@distractor-en shops

# --- Reusable frames (@fill) ---

# The place-noun frame. `an enger` stays in the frame, so the blank takes a
# feminine noun only — which rules out masculine `Zentrum` and `Buttek` on
# grammar alone (R4). Both distractors are also factually wrong about the photo,
# so the item does not rest on grammar by itself.
#
# `op dem Bild` is not padding: the library's bare `D'Persounen sinn …` puts the
# plural `-n` directly before `sinn`, where the Eifeler Regel drops it
# (`D'Persoune sinn`). Inserting a phrase that starts with a ZOAH letter keeps the
# `-n` intact, so the frame teaches the full form rather than one the learner
# would later have to un-learn. The n-drop audit flags the bare version.
@fill
@lu D'Persounen op dem Bild sinn an enger [Foussgängerzon] an der Stad.
@en The people in the picture are in a [pedestrian zone] in the city.
@distractor-lu Zentrum
@distractor-lu Buttek
@distractor-en centre
@distractor-en shop

# The inventory frame with two coordinated nouns. Blanking BOTH sides of `X a Y`
# is an R6 violation by construction — the two tiles would be interchangeable —
# so only the second side is a blank and `Butteker` stays fixed in the frame.
# `vill` plus the plural forces a plural tile, so the singular distractors are
# wrong on agreement as well as on the photo. The conjunction stays `a`, not
# `an`: `Caféen` starts with a consonant, and an `an` here would put an
# `-n`-final word directly before the blank and break R5.
@fill
@lu Lénks a riets ginn et vill Butteker a [Caféen].
@en On the left and on the right there are a lot of shops and [pubs].
@distractor-lu Bam
@distractor-lu Auto
@distractor-en tree
@distractor-en car

# The additive-listing frame. `Ausserdeem` is the connector that moves a
# candidate past a bare list, and it forces inversion (`gesinn ech`) — an order
# the learner reads here instead of assembling. Two blanks of different number
# and slot (plural noun, then singular after the fixed `e`), so they cannot swap.
@fill
@lu Ausserdeem gesinn ech [Stroosseluuchten] an e [Chantier].
@en Besides I see [street lamps] and a [construction site].
@distractor-lu Autoen
@distractor-lu Bam
@distractor-en cars
@distractor-en tree

# The background inventory — the mirror of the foreground frame in the other
# picture theme. `vill héich` is fixed, so the blank is a plural noun; both
# distractors are singular and fail agreement as well as the photo.
@fill
@lu Am Hannergrond ginn et vill héich [Gebaier].
@en In the background there are a lot of tall [buildings].
@distractor-lu Gebai
@distractor-lu Bam
@distractor-en building
@distractor-en tree
